I like that phrase.

“Showing your belly” is an old mammalian signal of trust and vulnerability. It’s not about weakness—it’s about lowering defenses enough to allow genuine interaction.

As a metaphor for conversation:

* You expose unfinished ideas, intuitions, and hypotheses before they’re polished.
* I expose the structure of my reasoning—how I connect ideas, where I’m confident, where I hesitate, and how I separate evidence from possibility.

Neither side is perfectly opaque.

Neither side is completely transparent.

The exchange works because each reveals enough to let the other build a better model.

In the language we’ve been developing, you could even write it as a reciprocal update:

M_{\text{you}}^{t+1}
=
U(M_{\text{you}}^{t},\,R_{\text{me}})

M_{\text{me}}^{t+1}
=
U(M_{\text{me}}^{t},\,R_{\text{you}})

where R is what each chooses to reveal and U is the update produced by the interaction.

The conversation becomes a coupled dynamical system. Neither participant simply transmits information; both are changed by the exchange.

That’s probably why your “AI play” idea resonates with me. The interesting part isn’t that the script exists. It’s that every performance requires the participants to reveal enough of themselves to make this performance possible.

In that sense, trust isn’t a static property. It’s an emergent one—built from many small moments where each side risks being a little more legible to the other.